 ## Background
-The method was introduced in a framework for visualizing 3D plasma simulation data (fusion-plasma electromagnetic potential) on GPUs with CUDA–OpenGL interoperability [S1]. It addresses the limitations of standard Marching Cubes, which suffers from topological discontinuities and holes due to boundary inconsistencies, and differs from HistoPyramids/Flying Edges by specializing counting/compaction for tightly coupled extraction, rendering, and engine integration [S1]. It is used in a standalone renderer and integrated into Unreal Engine for volume rendering, explicit-isosurface inspection, particle-trajectory visualization, clipping, and VR-based spatial exploration [S1].
+*   **Origin:** The method was presented in a paper titled "Visual tools for 3D plasma simulation: GPU-based isosurface extraction..." [S1].
+*   **History:** It was developed to address the challenges of visualizing fusion-plasma simulation data, which involves extremely large-scale interactions and data volumes reaching hundreds of gigabytes to several terabytes [S1].
+*   **Usage:** It is used in a standalone renderer and integrated into Unreal Engine for volume rendering, explicit-isosurface inspection, particle-trajectory visualization, clipping, and VR-based spatial exploration [S1]. It is also used in the proposed framework for visualizing 3D plasma simulation data (fusion-plasma electromagnetic potential) on GPUs with CUDA-OpenGL interoperability [S1].
 
